Sam should treat this as an evidence-and-deadline problem and avoid relying only on memory.

### 1. Check the tenancy agreement and local rules
Sam should look at the tenancy agreement for:

- the deposit amount and deduction rules;
- any requirement for move-in/move-out inspections;
- deadlines for the landlord to return the deposit or explain deductions;
- whether the deposit had to be held in a tenancy deposit scheme or similar local scheme.

The exact dispute route depends on the local jurisdiction, but most places require landlords to justify deductions and cannot charge for ordinary “wear and tear.”

### 2. Ask Lee for written proof
Sam should write to Lee and request:

- an itemized list of claimed deductions;
- receipts/quotes for repainting and carpet replacement;
- proof of the carpet’s age or installation date;
- any move-in inspection report, check-in inventory, photos, or condition notes;
- proof of how the deposit was protected, if that is required locally.

Sam should send this by email or another method that creates a record.

### 3. Gather Sam’s own evidence
Useful evidence includes:

- the professional cleaning receipt;
- dated photos/videos from before moving out;
- any photos from the start of the tenancy, listing photos, emails, texts, or witnesses;
- the tenancy agreement and rent/payment records;
- any prior complaints or repair requests.

Because there is no move-in inspection report, anything that helps show the flat’s original condition will be important.

### 4. Focus on “reasonable deductions,” not just full refund
Sam may not automatically get the full $1,800 if the red wine stains were tenant-caused damage. But Lee likely cannot keep the whole deposit simply because of a missing inspection report.

Key points:

- Repainting after a two-year tenancy is often normal wear and tear unless there was serious damage

